WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.860
Follow Langfocus on Facebook, Twitter and Instagram.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:10.060
Hello everyone. Welcome to Langfocus channel and my name is Paul.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:15.140
Today's topic is a language that some of you have probably never heard up but it's a fascinating topic

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:16.960
and one that definitely worth a look.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:18.660
The topic is Quechua.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:24.660
And in Quechua, the language that sometimes is referred to as "Runa Simi", which means "the people's language".

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:30.860
If we include all varieties of Quechua, it is by far the most widely spoken native language in South America.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:35.740
With between 8 and 10 millions speakers spread out across Peru, Bolivia and Ecuador,

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:38.680
as well as northern Argentina and southern Colombia.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:44.080
Spanish is now the dominant language in all those countries, but of course that wasn't always the case.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:48.780
Even if you haven't heard up Quechua, you must definitely heard up of Inca civilization

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.120
whose capital was located at Cusco, in present-day Peru.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:56.060
And whose empire was the largest in the Americas in the pre-colonial period.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:59.860
As their empire expanded, the language spread to new areas

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:05.160
because it became the "general language" (or the lingua franca) of the areas under the empire's control.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:10.260
The origin of Quechua is debated with some people saying that it originated in Cusco area

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:14.220
and other saying that it originated in central coastal area of Peru.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:17.960
Throughtout the time of Inca rule Quechua was not a written language.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:25.000
Records were only kept in quipus, a system of knotten strings that serve as symbols or mnemonic reminds of information.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:31.220
Quipus are not fully understood but they are not thought to represent the sounds or the structure of Quechua.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:35.780
Insteand they simply provide reminds of information to be passed down orally.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.520
Quechua did not become a written language until the Spanish conquest of the Inca Empire

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:43.940
which took place between 1532 and 1572.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:48.640
Quechua continue to be spoken as a lingua franca after the Spanish conquest

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:54.120
and the Catholic Church used Quechua at its efforts to convert people, spreading the language even further.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:02:00.420
The oldest written record of Quechua is a book written in 1560 by the missionary Domingo de Santo Tomás

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:05.780
called "Grammar or Art of the General Language of the Indians of the Royalty of Peru"

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:12.220
After the Tupac Amaru rebellion of 1780, the colonial power banned Quechua from public use.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:18.160
Even Quechua texts that were pro-colonial or pro-Catholic were banned and the language began to decline.

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:24.820
After the Spanish-American wars of Independence from 1808 to 1833, Quechua had a brief revival.

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:28.180
But it has gradually lost prestige and decline in use.

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:34.060
One of the main reasons for the decline of Quechua is the use of Spanish as the language of education and of the media

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:37.760
and the use of Spanish as the main language of the national economies.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:41.420
And with the economy development comes increased urbanization.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:44.200
When people moved from their local area to a major city

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:48.500
it's necessary to speak Spanish with the majority of people who don't speak Quechua.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:53.440
Spanish is often thought of as the language prestige and the economic opportunity

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:59.980
so speakers of Quechua or people whose parents speak Quechua often see it as something low prestige or something to avoid.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:06.040
Some Quechua-speaking parents avoid speaking Quechua with their children at home and instead speak to them in Spanish.

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:10.780
Even a reseaching this and then looking for volunteers to read Quechua samples,

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:15.780
some people told me that they themselves don't speak Quechua but their friends parents speak Quechua

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.160
or their uncle or aunt speak Quechua.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.640
Peru was the first country to make Quechua an official language in 1975

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:25.580
along with Aymara and other native languages.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:31.440
In Bolivia it's been an official language along with 35 other native languages since 2006

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:35.120
In Bolivia each regional government has officially bilingual

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:38.420
choosing both Spanish and one of the official languages.

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:42.980
In Ecuador Quechua has had semi-official status since 2009.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:50.120
Spanish is the main official language but Quechua and Shuar are reffered as official languages for intercultural relations.

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:56.060
Even though Quechua began to been written after the Spanish conquest is still mainly is spoken language

00:03:56.060 --> 00:04:00.800
and it isn't really written all that much and there are various different standards of orthography.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:04.080
This is partly because Quechua is spoken differently in different places.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.280
Quechua is not really a single language.

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:13.020
Rather it is a series of closely related groups of dialects

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:18.200
and those groups of dialects that have a high level of mutual intelligibility can be considered languages.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:24.360
If were a standard written language that was widely accepted and widely used throughtout all the different areas

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:28.540
then it might be able to be called a single language because that unifying force.

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:30.680
But there is no such standard language.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:35.940
Quechua is divided into two major dialect categories: Quechua I and Quechua II,

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:40.960
which are fairly distinct from each other but there's a dialect continuum within each category.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:46.320
Quechua I is spoken in central Peru, and is sometimes referred to as Waywash;

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:52.320
Quechua II, which is sometimes called Wampu, can be further subdivided into three dialect groups:

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:55.240
Quechua II-a, in northern Peru;

00:04:55.460 --> 00:05:02.460
Quechua II-b - Northern Quechua, also known as Kichwa, which is spoken mainly in Ecuador and southern Colombia;

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:09.240
and Quechua II-c - Souther Quechua, which include the Quechua spoken around Cusco, in Bolivia and in Argentina.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:16.400
Southern Quechua is the most commonly spoken, and the Cusco dialect is often seen as de facto standard variety of Quechua

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:17.860
even though it really isn't.

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:24.440
The dialects within each of those groups are largely intelligible and there is some degree of intelligibility between the different groups.

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:31.600
The lowest degree of intelligibility is between Quechua I and Quechua II, even if both varieties are located in Peru.

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:37.200
The difference between Quechua I and Quechua II seems to have resulted froma slipt from Proto-Quechua.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:40.800
The main differences between them are in vocabulary and morphology.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:46.120
There are differences in pronunciation too, but there are also pronunciation differences within each group.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.300
So, what is Quechua like?

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:52.240
Let's look at some of the features of Quechua focusing on Southern Quechua.

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:53.080
Syntax

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:57.120
Quechua is a SOV language, subject - object - verb.

00:05:57.560 --> 00:05:59.660
Let's take a look at an example sentence.

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:01.680
María wasita ruwan.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:04.040
This means "María buids a house".

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:09.800
Word for word it's: María - house - object marker - build - third person singular

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:13.080
You can see that the basic order is "Subject - Object - Verb".

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:16.920
The subjetc comes at the beginnig of the sentence when there's a explicit subject

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:21.160
but it's possible to have a complete sentence without the subject at the beginning like

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:22.300
Wasita ruwan

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:28.280
The end suffix at the end is a subject suffix so it's often not necessary to have a subject at the beginning.

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:29.960
Agglutination

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:33.540
One of Quechua's main feactures is that it's highly agglutinative.

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:38.500
Let's look at an example of agglutination using the noun Mishi means "cat".

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:40.000
Let's add the plural marker

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:40.520
kuna

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:41.840
Mishikuna

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:47.380
Means "cats" or "the cats". There's no definite article in Quechua, so it depends on the context.

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:49.060
Let's add the word for "with"

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:50.200
wan

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:51.680
Mishikunawan

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:52.980
Means "with the cats".

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:56.460
But let's move this over and add a different suffix meaning "your"

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:57.220
yki

00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:59.000
Mishiykikunawan

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:00.380
Means "with your cats".

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:02.560
And if we take away wan we have

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.520
Mishiykikuna

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:06.320
which simply means "your cats".

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:08.920
One more example, using the word for "road"

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:10.180
Ñan

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:12.720
Add the plural marker kuna, and we have

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:13.720
Ñankuna

00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:14.580
"roads"

00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:16.260
Then add the word for "on"

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:16.780
pi

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:17.860
Ñankunapi

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:19.060
Means "on the roads".

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:24.520
Let's remove the word of the end and slide this over and again we can add a possesive suffix, this time

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:25.120
nku

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:26.000
meaning "their".

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:30.940
But it's awkward to have these two nasal sounds right next to each other with no vowels, so you add

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:31.700
ni

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:33.720
an euphonic particle that has no meaning.

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:34.640
So we have

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:36.060
Ñanninkukuna

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:37.560
which means "their roads".

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:39.100
Then add the post position

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:39.600
pi

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:40.720
again and we have

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:42.280
Ñanninkukunapi

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:43.780
which means "on their roads".

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:48.380
Let's look at an example of agglutination using a verb.

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:49.580
We're starting with

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:50.440
Uyariy

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:53.100
the infinitive of the verb, meaning "to hear".

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:58.060
If we take away the y of the end, we now have the verb stem to which we can add suffixes.

00:07:58.460 --> 00:07:59.120
First add

00:07:59.120 --> 00:07:59.620
ni

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:02.400
a subject pronoun suffix meaning "I", so

00:08:02.540 --> 00:08:03.520
Uyarini

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:04.500
means "I hear".

00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:07.720
Let's move this over and we can add a past tense suffix to the stem.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:08.540
rqa

00:08:08.740 --> 00:08:09.520
Now we have

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:11.000
Uyarerqani

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:12.220
which means "I heard".

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:14.760
Another example meaning "I am learning Quechua".

00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:17.040
Runasimita yachakusani

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:20.580
Runasimi is the word of "Quechua", as I said earlier.

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:22.840
And ta is the object marker suffix

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:25.340
which shows that Runasimi is the object.

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:26.900
But let's focus on the verb

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:29.920
yacha is the stem of verb meaning "to know",

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:34.280
ku is a suffix which makes the verb reflexive or done to oneself

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:36.400
so yachaku means "learn",

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:40.620
sa is a progressive suffix which shows that the action is in progress now,

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:43.440
and ni is the subject pronoun suffix meaning "I".

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:49.760
One feature of Quechua that will be unfamiliar to speakers of many languages is topic and focus markers

00:08:50.020 --> 00:08:53.960
The topic marker indicates what the topic of theme of a sentence is.

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:59.260
The topic is always some information that has been previously mentioned, or something that is presupposed.

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:06.400
The focus marker indicates new information or information that is not presupposed, or it shows some point of contrast.

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:09.480
The topic marker is [ka] or [ha] (qa)

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:13.260
and the focus marker is [m] or [n], or mi.

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:14.600
Let's look at the sentence.

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:17.080
Pin wasita ruwarqan?

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:18.960
This means "Who built the house?"

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:26.760
Word for word it's: Who - focus marker - house - object marker - build - past tense - third person singular subject

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:32.480
Here the focus marker is attached to the question word to the focus on the new information that is being requested.

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:33.740
Here's a posible answer.

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:36.480
Pirdum wasitataqa ruwarqan

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:38.920
This means "it was Pirdu who built the house".

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:48.120
Word for word it's: Pirdu - focus marker - house - object marker - topic marker - build - past suffix - third personal singular subject

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:52.960
The focus marker is attached to Pirdu because "he" (Pirdu) is the new information being introduced.

00:09:53.140 --> 00:09:56.840
The topic marker is attached to "house", after the object marker

00:09:56.840 --> 00:10:01.420
because "house" is the ongoing theme that was already introduce earlier in the conversation.

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:04.020
Evidential markers

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:10.200
That focus markers has another function to show direct evidence that something is true.

00:10:10.680 --> 00:10:14.940
This is a kind of evidential, an indicator of the source of information.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:16.940
Another evidential marker is

00:10:16.940 --> 00:10:17.580
cha

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:18.980
which shows inference,

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:19.860
shi

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:20.740
or [sh]

00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:23.580
which shows hearsay, something that you heard.

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:25.440
Let's look at the example sentences.

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:26.780
Paramushanmi

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:28.160
This means "it is raining".

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:29.220
Word for word it's:

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:37.880
Rain - cislocative particle which indicates movement towards the speaker - progressive suffix - third person singular subject - evidential marker

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:43.100
This evidential mi indicates that the speaker has observed firsthand that it's raining.

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:47.220
The next sentence is exactly except for the evidential marker at the end.

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:48.780
Paramushancha

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:51.400
This means something like "I think it is raining".

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:56.040
The marker cha indicates that the speaker in first or guesses that it's raining.

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:57.020
The next sentence

00:10:57.140 --> 00:10:58.240
Paramushanshi

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:00.440
Means something like "I heard it is raining".

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:05.720
The evidential particle shi indicates that the speaker has been told by someone that it's raining

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:07.760
but they have no firsthand evidence of it.

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:09.740
Spanish loanwords

00:11:10.040 --> 00:11:13.040
Quechua uses a large number of loanwords of Spanish

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:18.200
as much as one third of Quechua vocabulary depending of the variety of Quechua and the area.

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:19.480
Here are some examples:

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:20.280
Sirbisa

00:11:20.380 --> 00:11:21.900
from Spanish cerveza.

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:22.720
Chufir

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:25.140
from Spanish chofer, meaning "driver".

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:26.040
Escuela

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:27.500
the Spanish word for "school".

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:28.400
And libro

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:31.120
the Spanish word for "book", instead of Quechua word

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:31.680
Patara

00:11:32.420 --> 00:11:37.420
As you can see, Quechua is a fascinating language and one that is deeply connected with the history

00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:41.020
and culture of the speakers, and of the countries where is spoken.

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:46.160
The decline of Quechua is unfortunate and the loss of Quechua would be a very sad thing indeed.

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:52.080
Perhaps the key to preserving Quechua is truly bilingual schooling and more focus on Quechua illiteracy.

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:53.420
The Question of the Day

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:55.960
If there are any native speakers of Quechua watching:

00:11:55.960 --> 00:12:00.980
How often do you speak Quechua, and how often do you speak Spanish? Does it depend on the situation?

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:05.820
And for other people: Do you notice any features of Quechua that you've noticed in other languages?

00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:09.900
